w no clear change and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.84\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.52 to +1.16). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 4 of 12 months. Highs rose in none.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Victoria's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.88 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 40.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 2004 (73.65 in); the driest, 2011 (13.08 in). Victoria almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.5 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 4 daily record highs, 4 daily record lows"}}